# SHIFT 3 items (Tuesday 11/03 0700 to 1900, day shift, J. Morales RN). Exec'd with E and parts available.
items3 = []

items3.append({'id': '3.1', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1, 'lines': 11, 'title': 'The 0800 Sedation Interruption',
               'prompt': 'Set the clock to 0700 and read the 0850 nursing note and the 0845 respiratory therapy note. (a) What was done at 0800 and what was the order for the sedatives? (b) How did Marcus respond (RASS, commands, CAM-ICU, his mother)? (c) Name two findings that would make you stop the interruption early and restart the sedation. (d) Why is he NOT having a breathing trial (SBT) today even though FiO2 is 0.40 and PEEP is 5?',
               'where': 'Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 0850, Respiratory Therapy Note 0845, ICU Progress Note 0715); Orders (SAT); MAR (propofol)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Propofol held at 0800 (was 30 mcg/kg/min) while fentanyl 100 mcg/h continued for pain; the order says wake to RASS 0 to -1 or until agitation or distress, then restart at half the prior rate (restarted 0835 at 20 mcg/kg/min, back to 30 at 0930).', 0.25, 'Orders, MAR, note'),
                   E('Woke to voice within minutes, RASS 0, followed commands with the LEFT hand and left leg, mouthed "mom", tearful, calmed holding his mother Linda\'s hand; CAM-ICU negative; HR 92-98, MAP 78-82, SpO2 98%, no dyssynchrony.', 0.25, 'Nursing note 0850'),
                   E('Any two stop criteria: agitation or distress, pulling at the tube or lines, ventilator dyssynchrony or biting, SpO2 under about 88-90%, sustained HR/BP change, respiratory rate over about 35, seizure or new arrhythmia, unsafe pain.', 0.25, ''),
                   E('Still in the post-resuscitation fluid-shift phase (albumin 5% running until 1000, Parkland ended only at 0130), airway/facial edema, no cuff-leak test, a 1400 dressing change needing sedation and analgesia; reassess tomorrow (Dr. Brandt and RT).', 0.25, 'Progress note, RT note')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.2', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1, 'lines': 10, 'title': 'Albumin and the Feeds',
               'prompt': 'Albumin 5% 500 mL runs from 0600 to 1000. (a) Calculate the pump rate in mL/h. (b) Why is albumin given to Marcus now (use a lab value), and what two things do you assess during the infusion? (c) What did the LR rate change to at 1000? (d) The dietitian wrote about the tube feeding and the 1400 dressing change: what did she recommend about holding the feeding?',
               'where': 'MAR (albumin 5%, lactated Ringer\'s); Lab Results (Albumin); Nursing Note 1030; Nutrition Follow-Up Note 1045; Flowsheets > Intake/Output',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('500 mL / 4 h = 125 mL/h.', 0.25, ''),
                   E('Serum albumin 2.6 (low) and ongoing capillary leak: albumin holds fluid in the vessels, so less crystalloid is needed (he is already about 12% over the Parkland goal). Assess for fluid overload (lung sounds, SpO2, ventilator pressures, edema) and for reaction (fever, chills, hypotension, rash) plus urine output.', 0.5, 'Labs, notes'),
                   E('LR went from 75 mL/h to 100 mL/h at 1000 (titrate urine output 0.5-1 mL/kg/h).', 0.125, 'MAR'),
                   E('Do NOT hold the feeding (Osmolite 1.5 at goal 70 mL/h) for dressing changes; hold only for the OR or extubation (and she added a protein modular 15 g twice a day).', 0.125, 'RD note')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.3', 'type': 'table', 'points': 1.5, 'title': 'Noon Electrolytes and the Replacement Orders',
               'prompt': 'Open the 1200 labs, the orders and the MAR. For each result, write the value, the replacement ordered (drug, dose, route, rate) with the time you gave it, and the safety check or monitoring you do. Then answer below the table: why did the potassium and phosphorus drop when he is on an insulin infusion and tube feeding, and what other 1200 result could the phosphate make worse?',
               'where': 'Lab Results (Chemistry, 1200); Orders; MAR (potassium chloride, magnesium sulfate, sodium phosphate); Nursing Note 1240',
               'cols': ['Result (1200)', 'Replacement ordered and time given', 'Safety check / what you monitor'], 'widths': [1.5, 2.8, 2.7], 'rows': [['Potassium'], ['Magnesium'], ['Phosphorus']], 'rowH': 1.0,
               'key': {'answer': 'Rows 0.4 each (value 0.1, order and time 0.15, safety 0.15) = 1.2; the two sentences below the table 0.3.', 'bank': True,
                       'elements': [
                           E('Potassium 3.6 (goal 4.0 or higher): KCl 20 mEq in 100 mL over 2 h (10 mEq/h) through the central line, given 1230 and a second dose 1430. Safety: central line only, maximum 10 mEq/h, telemetry, separate pump, double check; hold and call for urine output under 30 mL/h for 2 hours; recheck K 4 h later.', 0.4, 'Orders, MAR'),
                           E('Magnesium 1.7: magnesium sulfate 2 g in 50 mL over 1 h IV, given 1330. Safety: watch BP, HR and reflexes/respiration (hypotension, flushing); recheck level with the evening labs.', 0.4, 'MAR'),
                           E('Phosphorus 2.1 (low): sodium phosphate 15 mmol in 250 mL over 4 h via the central line, started 1500. Safety: slow infusion, separate pump, watch calcium (7.6, low) and renal function; recheck with the evening labs.', 0.4, 'MAR'),
                           E('Insulin and feeding (refeeding) drive K and phosphorus into the cells, plus burn and fluid losses and dilution. Phosphate binds calcium and can lower an already low calcium (Ca 7.6): watch for tingling, cramps, QT change and ask about the calcium.', 0.3, '')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.4', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1, 'lines': 11, 'title': 'The 1400 Dressing Change',
               'prompt': 'You and N. Ibarra, RN (burn nurse specialist) change the dressings. Read the 1425 and 1455 notes. (a) List the four NEW findings that told you this is not a normal burn wound. (b) What pain medicine was given, when, and how did CPOT change? (c) What did you do to the edge of the red area at 1430 and why? (d) Which finding at the right chest border is the most worrisome for tissue death?',
               'where': 'Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 1425, Wound Care Specialist Note 1455); MAR (hydromorphone); Flowsheets (CPOT)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('New since Monday: purulent drainage (thick yellow-green) on the chest/arm dressings; foul odor; erythema spreading beyond the burn margin (about 2-3 cm in the notes, 4 cm on the 1430 flowsheet entry; warm, indurated); eschar separating with a dark violaceous area. (Any four of these, including increased pain with cleansing.)', 0.5, 'Notes'),
                   E('Hydromorphone 0.5 mg IV at 1345, 15 minutes before the change; CPOT 2/8 at 1300, 5/8 during dressing removal, back to 4 by 1500.', 0.25, 'MAR'),
                   E('Marked the margin of erythema with a skin marker (dated and timed 1430), measured and photographed, to see whether it spreads; recheck every 4 hours and every shift.', 0.125, ''),
                   E('The dark violaceous, non-blanching area about 4 x 5 cm at the right chest border with loss of eschar adherence: possible invasive infection with tissue necrosis (needs surgeon exam and biopsy).', 0.125, '')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.5', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 14, 'title': 'Analyze the Graph: Temperature, Heart Rate and MAP',
               'prompt': 'Study the figure (it is drawn from the flowsheet for 0700 to 1900). (a) At about what time did the change begin and which of the three values moved first? (b) Describe the pattern at 1400, 1700 and 1900 in your own words and say what it means for Marcus. (c) After the norepinephrine starts at 1730 the MAP climbs back up: what does the graph tell you about whether he is getting better? (d) Burn patients often have a fast heart rate and a temperature near 100 F even when they are well: why are the usual SIRS criteria unreliable for him, and name two signs you use instead.',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs (Tuesday 0700-1900); Notes (Critical Care Event Note 1755); the figure below',
               'images': [{'path': 'figs/fig_s3_vitals.png', 'caption': 'Figure: Marcus Delaney, temperature, heart rate and MAP, Tuesday 0700 to 1900.', 'width': 6.2}],
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Stable all morning (T 99.4-99.7, HR 92-98, MAP 77-80); the change starts about 1300 (T 99.9, a slow creep from 1100), and the sharp bend is between 1300 and 1400. Temperature moved first (slowly, from 99.7 to 99.9); then HR (97 to 105) and MAP (77 to 72) changed together at 1400.', 0.5, 'Graph, vitals'),
                   E('Steady rise of temperature (100.4 at 1400, 101.6 at 1700, 102.3 at 1900) and HR (105, 122, 130) with falling MAP (72, 66, then 63 at 1715, below the goal of 65): the picture of evolving sepsis from the wound infection, going into septic shock (vasodilation and low perfusion); it is a trend, not a single value.', 0.5, 'Graph'),
                   E('MAP recovers (64 to 70) because the drug squeezes the vessels, but temperature and heart rate keep climbing (HR 130, T 102.3), so the infection is not controlled; he is not better, the pressor treats the number, not the cause (needs source control: antibiotics, surgery, fluids carefully).', 0.25, 'Graph'),
                   E('He is already hypermetabolic after the burn (baseline HR above 90 and temperature 99-100 F, tachypnea, high WBC and glucose), so SIRS criteria are met by almost every burn patient and do not separate sepsis. Better: trend (progressive tachycardia over 110, temperature over about 102 F/39 C, RR over 25), new hyperglycemia/insulin resistance (glucose 205-244), falling platelets, feeding intolerance, falling urine output, rising lactate, wound changes. (Accept any two.)', 0.25, 'Event note 1755')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.6', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'The SBAR Call at 1540',
               'prompt': 'It is 1540. You are going to call Dr. Okafor and Dr. Brandt. Write what you say (S, B, A, R) using the chart numbers at 1530 and the trend since 1300. End with what you ask them for: name at least three orders or decisions you request.',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Vital Signs, Intake/Output, Lab Results; Notes (Nursing Note 1550 Sepsis SBAR)',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Situation: bed/name, suspected burn wound infection with sepsis; 1530 T 101.1 F, HR 113, BP 105/51 (MAP 69), RR 23, SpO2 96% on the ventilator.', 0.375, 'Vitals'),
                   E('Background: 34-year-old man, 22% TBSA flame burn with inhalation injury, post-burn day 2, intubated, NKDA, full code; 1400 dressing change findings (purulent drainage, foul odor, spreading erythema, violaceous eschar at the right chest border).', 0.375, 'Notes'),
                   E('Assessment: trend T 99.9 (1300), 100.4, 100.9, 101.1; HR 97, 105, 108, 113; MAP 77, 72, 71, 69; RR 18 to 23; urine 45, 41, 36 mL/h (0.44 mL/kg/h, falling); glucose 205 at 1530 (was 173); WBC 15.4 and lactate 1.4 earlier; CPOT 4. "I am concerned this is early sepsis."', 0.375, 'Flowsheets'),
                   E('Recommendation: bedside wound exam by the surgeon; sepsis order set: cultures before antibiotics (blood x2, wound, tracheal aspirate, urine), lactate, procalcitonin, CBC with differential, coags; broad-spectrum antibiotics after cultures; fluids or MAP plan; call parameters (MAP under 65, urine under 30 mL/h). (Any three requests.)', 0.375, 'Note 1550')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.7', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 13, 'title': 'The Sepsis Bundle: Cultures and Lactate',
               'prompt': 'Open the 1655 nursing note and Lab Results. (a) List the four culture sites (five specimens) and the time collected, and which two blood sites. (b) Why must the cultures be drawn BEFORE the antibiotics, and when is it OK to give antibiotics without waiting? (c) Write the lactate values and times from 1555 to 1700 and what they mean. (d) What is the goal for giving antibiotics after recognizing sepsis, and what were Marcus\'s actual times?',
               'where': 'Chart Review > Notes (Nursing Note 1655, Burn Surgery Event Note 1645); Lab Results (Microbiology, Lactate, Procalcitonin); MAR',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Collected 1550: blood culture set 1 peripheral (left hand), set 2 from the left IJ central line, wound swab (right chest/arm, after cleansing, from the drainage), tracheal aspirate, urine from the Foley port; all labeled and sent. (Wound biopsy 1635 by Dr. Okafor.)', 0.5, 'Note 1655'),
                   E('Antibiotics kill or suppress the organisms and a culture drawn afterward can be falsely negative, so you could not target or narrow therapy; draw before the first dose, but in septic shock the antibiotics must not be delayed (goal within 1 hour); if cultures cannot be obtained quickly, give the antibiotics and document why.', 0.5, ''),
                   E('Lactate 2.9 at 1555 (high), 3.8 at 1700 (rising): tissue hypoperfusion (anaerobic metabolism); the rise despite maintenance fluids (the first bolus did not start until 1700) means he is getting worse, repeat in 2 hours (goal clearance 10-20% every 2 hours). Also WBC 18.4, procalcitonin 4.8 at 1555.', 0.25, 'Labs'),
                   E('Antibiotics within 1 hour of recognition: recognized and SBAR 1540, cultures 1550, pip-tazo 1630 (about 50 minutes after the call), vancomycin 1645 (about 65 minutes).', 0.25, 'MAR')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.8', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 14, 'title': 'Checking the Antibiotic Orders',
               'prompt': 'Before you hang the antibiotics you check the orders with pharmacy (A. Chen, PharmD). Marcus weighs 82 kg. (a) The vancomycin load is 20 mg/kg: calculate the dose and compare it with the 1,750 mg on the MAR, then give the pump rate in mg/h for the 2-hour infusion and the order\'s maximum rate. (b) The piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g in 100 mL is an extended infusion: over how long, at what mL/h, and why is it given this way? (c) What does the pip-tazo order say about running it with the vancomycin, and what will you do with the lumens of the left IJ? (d) Name two lab values you follow with this combination and why.',
               'where': 'Orders (piperacillin-tazobactam, vancomycin); MAR; Lab Results (Creatinine); Nursing Note 1655',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('20 mg/kg x 82 kg = 1,640 mg; the 1,750 mg is 21.3 mg/kg, about 7% above 1,640 mg (the order says "about 20 mg/kg, rounded to 1,750 mg", the nearest 250 mg; pharmacy verified). 1,750 mg / 2 h = 875 mg/h, under the order maximum of 1 g/h (slow the rate if red man syndrome).', 0.5, 'Orders, note'),
                   E('Infuse over 4 hours: 100 mL / 4 h = 25 mL/h. Extended infusion keeps the drug level above the organism MIC for longer (time-dependent beta-lactam), which helps in sepsis and with Pseudomonas.', 0.375, 'Order'),
                   E('The order says to give pip-tazo through its own lumen when possible, separate from vancomycin, and that the combination is associated with kidney injury (pharmacy also advises no co-infusion because compatibility depends on the concentration); run them on separate lumens/pumps of the left IJ (the norepinephrine later gets its own lumen too, never shared), and ask pharmacy if lumens run short.', 0.375, 'Order, note'),
                   E('Creatinine (1.1 at 1700; vancomycin and pip-tazo are nephrotoxic, and his urine output is falling) and vancomycin levels (pharmacy AUC dosing); also watch WBC/temperature for response and check the cultures to narrow in 48 hours.', 0.25, 'Labs')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.9', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1.5, 'lines': 15, 'title': 'Low MAP and Norepinephrine',
               'prompt': 'At 1715 the BP is 101/44 (MAP 63-64) after the 1700 LR bolus; the second bolus is given at 1745 and norepinephrine 16 mcg/mL is started at 1730 at 0.05 mcg/kg/min. (a) Calculate the pump rate in mL/h for 0.05 mcg/kg/min (82 kg), and the rate at 0.10 mcg/kg/min that you reached at 1830. (b) Where must it run and why (name the complication and one sign)? (c) How and how fast do you titrate, and what is the MAP goal and how is the MAP measured? (d) Why do the providers want to limit more fluid boluses?',
               'where': 'Orders (norepinephrine, LR bolus); MAR; Nursing Note 1755; Critical Care Event Note 1755; Flowsheets > Vital Signs',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('0.05 x 82 = 4.1 mcg/min x 60 = 246 mcg/h / 16 mcg/mL = 15.4 mL/h. At 0.10: 8.2 mcg/min = 492 mcg/h = 30.8 mL/h (as printed on the order).', 0.5, 'Order'),
                   E('Central line only, on a dedicated lumen with nothing else in it (never a PIV): extravasation causes tissue necrosis; check the site and the hand/extremity every hour (pain, blanching, swelling, cool skin, loss of blood return); if suspected stop the drip and notify the provider at once (the provider may order phentolamine; follow the unit extravasation protocol).', 0.375, 'Order'),
                   E('Titrate by 0.02 mcg/kg/min every 5 minutes (order; max 0.5 without attending approval) to MAP 65 or higher; wean by 0.02 every 15-30 minutes when MAP is over 75; MAP read from the left radial arterial line (leveled and zeroed); give a second RN check for each change; she went 0.05, 0.08 (1800), 0.10 (1830).', 0.375, 'Order, MAR'),
                   E('He is already about 12% over the Parkland total with moderate edema, the rising airway pressures (PIP 24 to 28, Pplat 17 to 21) and SpO2 falling from 98% to 95% suggest leak into the lungs, and the boluses lifted MAP only briefly; more fluid worsens edema and ARDS risk. Give vasopressor instead.', 0.25, 'Event note')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.10', 'type': 'box', 'points': 1, 'lines': 10, 'title': 'Switching the Topical Antimicrobial',
               'prompt': 'Silver sulfadiazine was stopped and mafenide acetate 5% soaks were started at 1730. (a) Why did the burn surgeon choose mafenide for this wound? (b) Name the two things you monitor with mafenide and look at the 1700 bicarbonate and the 1845 arterial blood gas: what do they show and why can you not blame the mafenide alone? (c) What do you give before each soak and how often are they done?',
               'where': 'Orders (mafenide, silver sulfadiazine); MAR; Lab Results (Chemistry 1700, Blood Gas 1845, Lactate); Burn Surgery Event Note 1645',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Mafenide penetrates eschar and wound tissue (the infection is under separating eschar), unlike silver sulfadiazine which does not; it is active against the Gram-negatives and many organisms in the wound.', 0.25, 'Event note'),
                   E('Monitor metabolic acidosis (mafenide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor: BMP/bicarbonate and ABG) and pain/burning on application. Chart: CO2 23 (1200) to 22 (1700); ABG 1845 pH 7.31, HCO3 18, base excess -8, PaCO2 36. This is a real metabolic acidosis, but lactate was already 3.8 at 1700 (septic shock) so the cause is mainly lactic acidosis, and mafenide can add to it: report it and trend.', 0.5, 'Labs'),
                   E('Pain medicine (hydromorphone/fentanyl) before the soak; soaks every 8 hours (next 0130), keep the gauze moist.', 0.25, 'Order')]}})

items3.append({'id': '3.11', 'type': 'choice', 'style': 'multi', 'points': 1, 'title': 'Falling Urine Output',
               'prompt': 'The urine output was 45, 41, 36, 28, 24 and 20 mL per hour from 1200 to 1800 (his goal is 0.5 mL/kg/h = 41 mL/h, and Foley care is done). At 1800 the output is 20 mL (0.24 mL/kg/h). Which actions are correct for you now?',
               'where': 'Flowsheets > Intake/Output and Vital Signs; Nursing Note 1240 (potassium plan); MAR; Lab Results (Creatinine); Notes 1755',
               'options': ['Report it to Dr. Brandt and Dr. Okafor now (it is below the 30 mL/h call limit) and tie it to the MAP and lactate.',
                           'Check the Foley for kinks, a full bag tubing loop or sediment, and the bladder pressure (last 12 mmHg).',
                           'Ask the provider whether to give a third 500 mL LR bolus because he is oliguric, without looking at his lungs or total fluid balance.',
                           'Hold any further potassium replacement and tell the provider (your 1240 plan was to hold potassium for urine output under 30 mL/h for 2 hours; it has been under 30 since 1500).',
                           'Ask about the doses of nephrotoxic drugs (vancomycin, pip-tazo) with pharmacy and follow the creatinine.',
                           'Stop the norepinephrine to see if the urine improves.',
                           'Keep recording hourly urine output and total I&O; remember he is also getting feeds and fluids, so urine output is the better marker.',
                           'Give furosemide to make urine because the output is low.'],
               'key': {'answer': ['Report it to Dr. Brandt and Dr. Okafor now...', 'Check the Foley for kinks...', 'Hold any further potassium replacement and tell the provider...', 'Ask about the doses of nephrotoxic drugs...', 'Keep recording hourly urine output and total I&O...'],
                       'rules': 'About 0.2 per correct option selected (5 correct = 1.0); subtract 0.2 for each wrong option selected (not below 0). Wrong: asking for another bolus without assessing (he is 12% over Parkland, edema, new lung findings and rising pressures; the oliguria is from low perfusion/sepsis), stopping the norepinephrine (MAP 63-64 would fall), a diuretic in septic shock (would worsen perfusion and kidney injury). By 1900 the chart totals for the shift are 4,094 in, 474 out, +3,620 net.',
                       'errors': 'Treating the number (more fluid or a diuretic) without looking for the cause, or not reporting it.'}})

items3.append({'id': '3.12', 'type': 'box', 'points': 2, 'lines': 16, 'title': 'Handoff at 1850',
               'prompt': 'It is 1850. Write your SBAR handoff for E. Haddad, RN, the night nurse. Use real numbers from your shift, say what is changing, and list what the night nurse must do or follow up in the next hours (look at the orders, MAR and labs).',
               'where': 'Everything charted this shift; Orders; MAR; Lab Results; Flowsheets (Vital Signs, Intake/Output); Nursing Note 1850',
               'key': {'elements': [
                   E('Situation: septic shock from a right chest/arm burn wound infection; 1830 T 102.1 F, HR 126, BP 108/48 (MAP 68), RR 29, SpO2 94%; on norepinephrine 0.10 mcg/kg/min (30.8 mL/h) to keep MAP 65 or higher; full code, NKDA.', 0.5, 'Vitals, note'),
                   E('Background: 22% TBSA flame burn with inhalation injury, post-burn day 2, intubated; Parkland ended 0130; wound infection found at 1400; cultures x4 sites 1550; pip-tazo 1630 (extended, 4 h) and vancomycin 1,750 mg 1645 (until about 1845) on separate lumens; LR 500 mL x2 (1700, 1745); norepinephrine started 1730 (0.05, 0.08 at 1800, 0.10 at 1830); mafenide started 1730, silver sulfadiazine stopped; biopsy 1635.', 0.5, 'MAR'),
                   E('Assessment: lactate 2.9 then 3.8 (repeat due 1900); ABG 1845 pH 7.31, HCO3 18, BE -8; urine 20 mL/h (0.24 mL/kg/h); glucose 244 on insulin 4 units/h (feeds still 70 mL/h); ventilator changed at 1800 to PEEP 8, FiO2 0.50, rate 18, PIP 29/Pplat 22 (early ARDS physiology, portable chest x-ray); propofol 40, fentanyl 150, RASS -3; WBC 18.4, procalcitonin 4.8, platelets 171, INR 1.4. Day shift I&O (EHR): 4,094 in, 474 out, +3,620 net.', 0.5, 'Labs, I&O'),
                   E('Recommendation: titrate norepinephrine for MAP 65 or higher and call Dr. Brandt if the dose keeps rising (vasopressin above 0.25, hydrocortisone considered); lactate at 1900 and ABG; hourly glucose and urine output (call for under 20 mL/h); follow cultures and biopsy; mafenide soaks every 8 hours (next 0130) with pain premedication and acid-base watch; re-mark the erythema margin every 4 hours; hourly right-hand neurovascular checks; recheck K, Mg and phosphorus; central line and extravasation checks; family at the bedside.', 0.5, 'Note 1850')]}})

parts.append({'id': '3', 'title': 'Shift 3: Tuesday 11/03 0700 to 1900 (day shift)', 'step': 'Day 2: wake-up, wound infection, sepsis', 'minutes': 60,
              'intro': 'Set the simulation clock to 0700 on Tuesday 11/03/26. You are J. Morales, RN, and you take report from the night nurse. Keep reading forward in the chart as the clock moves; do not look past 1900 for this shift. A quiet morning is followed by a change in condition in the afternoon.',
              'keyNote': 'Shift 3 follows the safe wake-up, the electrolyte orders, early recognition of burn wound infection (graph analysis, SBAR), the sepsis bundle with antibiotic checks, the norepinephrine math and the shock plan. A careful student should earn about 12 of the 16 points.',
              'items': items3})
